        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 464.24633333333327,
            "to": 474.24633333333327
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Neostigmine reverses non-depolarising neuromuscular blockade by raising acetylch</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Neostigmine reverses non-depolarising neuromuscular blockade by raising acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction. Why must it always be paired with glycopyrrolate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>To prevent tachycardia from excess nicotinic stimulation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Neostigmine raises acetylcholine at muscarinic sites too, causing bradycardia — not tachycardia. Glycopyrrolate blocks these muscarinic effects.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>To prevent muscarinic side effects such as bradycardia and excessive secretions</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Neostigmine inhibits acetylcholinesterase at both nicotinic and muscarinic receptors. Glycopyrrolate selectively blocks the unwanted muscarinic effects — bradycardia, bronchospasm, and hypersalivation — while preserving the desired neuromuscular reversal.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>To accelerate the hepatic metabolism of the non-depolarising agent</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Glycopyrrolate is an antimuscarinic agent and does not alter drug metabolism. Its role is to counteract the muscarinic side effects produced by elevated acetylcholine.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>To prevent recurrence of neuromuscular blockade after initial reversal</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Glycopyrrolate does not prevent recurarisation. Its sole purpose here is to block the muscarinic side effects — bradycardia, secretions, and bronchospasm — that neostigmine produces.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Neostigmine raises acetylcholine at ALL cholinergic sites, not just the neuromuscular junction. Glycopyrrolate blocks the muscarinic side effects (bradycardia, secretions, bronchospasm) while preserving the nicotinic reversal at the junction."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. Understanding why neostigmine needs an antimuscarinic partner is essential — acetylcholinesterase inhibition is non-selective, and unblocked muscarinic effects can cause dangerous bradycardia."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
