          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>During stable maintenance of general anaesthesia, a patient's previously normal </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>During stable maintenance of general anaesthesia, a patient's previously normal capnography trace suddenly goes flat. What does this pattern most urgently indicate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bronchospasm</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bronchospasm produces a characteristic shark-fin (upsloping) waveform due to uneven alveolar emptying, not a flat trace.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Oesophageal intubation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A flat trace immediately after intubation suggests oesophageal placement. During stable maintenance — when the trace was previously normal — sudden flattening points to a catastrophic drop in pulmonary blood flow.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cardiac arrest or massive pulmonary embolism</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. A sudden loss of the capnography waveform during stable maintenance indicates that CO₂ is no longer reaching the lungs — most urgently from cardiac arrest or massive pulmonary embolism. Immediate resuscitation is required.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Excessive depth of anaesthesia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Deep anaesthesia alters respiratory rate and tidal volume but does not cause a sudden flat capnography trace. A flat trace during stable maintenance signals a catastrophic loss of pulmonary blood flow.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "During stable maintenance, a suddenly flat capnography trace means CO₂ is no longer reaching the lungs. The most urgent causes are cardiac arrest and massive pulmonary embolism — both require immediate resuscitation."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Capnography is often called the stethoscope of anaesthesia — the waveform shape tells you what the number alone cannot. A sudden flat trace demands immediate assessment for cardiac arrest or massive PE."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
